Part A: Theory (30-40 min)

1. Docker network modes:

bridge (default): a virtual switch (docker0) on the host. Containers get private IPs (172.17.x.x) and reach the outside via NAT.
host: the container shares the host’s network stack (no isolation).
none: no networking.

2. Port publishing. -p HOST:CONTAINER creates iptables DNAT rules so outside traffic to the host port reaches the container.

3. User-defined bridge networks give DNS by container name. On the default bridge, containers can’t resolve each other by name; on a custom network they can. This foreshadows Kubernetes Services and DNS in Week 3.

4. Volumes and mounts:

Named volume: managed by Docker (/var/lib/docker/volumes); best for databases.
Bind mount: maps a host directory into the container; good for development.
tmpfs: memory only.

5. Why one host isn’t enough. After today you can run multi-container apps on one machine. Now ask what happens when:

The host crashes (everything is down)
You need more CPU than one machine has
Containers on different machines must communicate
You want to update with zero downtime
You must decide which host runs which container

You’d have to script all of this by hand. That gap is exactly what Kubernetes fills.
